@benitoz It sounds like you’re saying it’s “too big to fail.” Is that right?

But should America destroy its own industries to protect Americans’ wealth?

Wealth can be rebuilt, but once an industry is lost, it may never be recovered.

@alexeheath's latest reporting on Meta AI:

“He expects to surpass Opus-level performance this year with the big frontier model Meta is training, codenamed Watermelon.”

$META https://t.co/6DVPZihyB7

@teortaxesTex Isn’t that a bit too idealistic as a counterargument?

If GPU scarcity is pushing them toward open source, doesn’t that also mean GPUs for training are scarce as well?

And if that’s the case, then the cost of building LLMs themselves is going up.

@colne_e その点には同意します。しかし、フィジカルAI(物理AI)を訓練する際にも、膨大なGPUとコンピューティング資源が必要になります。そして、フィジカルAIに必要なモデルは、日本が自ら作り上げるべきだと私は考えています。
また、私は日本企業こそ競い合うようにフィジカルAIへ参入すべきだと思っています。現在、韓国の財閥は、普段はロボットと縁のない企業までもがこぞってフィジカルAIに乗り出そうと大騒ぎしている状況です。
これはバブルのように見えるかもしれませんが、産業の黎明期においては極めてポジティブなことです。競争を通じてこそ技術は進歩するのですから。

@Alex_Intel_ This is a double-edged sword.

One thought that now comes to mind is that the U.S. may eventually only allow N-1 generation semiconductors to be exported to countries outside the U.S.

For example, Rubin could be supplied only within the U.S., while other countries would only receive Blackwell.

@KairosPraxis What I mean is that not every country occupies a bottleneck position in the AI supply chain the way Korea or Taiwan does.

And the same applies to Korea and Taiwan as well. The very fact that there is now a visible possibility that the U.S. could cut off access to powerful LLMs for Korea creates uncertainty in itself.

Because of this, every country will rush into sovereign AI.
